@@s001 Mergesort: sorting by dividing
@@s002 dato:not yet sorted,ora:being worked on,ok:sorted
@@s003 Input: 8 numbers. Mergesort: <b>divide</b> in half, <b>sort</b> recursively the two halves, <b>merge</b> the two sorted lists.
@@s004 I divide into <b>[5 2 4 7]</b> and <b>[1 3 2 6]</b>. Each half is a problem of the same type, smaller.
@@s005 Again: <b>[5 2] [4 7] [1 3] [2 6]</b>. Two numbers are already easy: a pair is sorted with one comparison.
@@s006 I <b>merge</b> the pairs: [2 5] [4 7] [1 3] [2 6]. Each merge scans the two lists with two indices: linear cost.
@@s007 I merge two by two: [2 4 5 7] and [1 2 3 6]. Each level of the recursion does <b>O(n)</b> of work in total.
@@s008 Last merge: 1 2 2 3 4 5 6 7. Three levels, each O(n): <b>O(n log n)</b>. It is the recurrence <code>T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n</code>.
@@s009 Mergesort: sorting by dividing
@@s010 Mergesort is the template of every divide and conquer with linear recombination: divide in half, sort the halves, merge the two sorted lists.
@@s011 The idea
@@s012 Problem: sort <code>n</code> elements. Idea: divide in half, sort the two halves recursively, then <b>merge</b> the two sorted lists. If <code>n ≤ 1</code> the array is already sorted.
@@s013 Mergesort on the example of the guide
@@s014 The merge
@@s015 Two lists already sorted are merged with two indices: you compare the first elements, you move the smaller into the output, and advance by one in its list. Each element is moved once only: the merge costs <code>O(n)</code>.
@@s016 The cost
@@s017 Recurrence: <code>T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n)</code>. The master theorem gives <code>O(n log n)</code>. Intuitively: each level of the recursion does <code>O(n)</code> of work in total, and there are <code>log n</code> levels.
@@s018 The code, side by side
@@s019 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
